    "final_text": "I fixed all 5 bugs in `src/cart.js`. All 43 tests in `tests/cart.test.js` pass on the fixed code; 38 of them failed on the original. Run them with `npm test`. There are no dependencies to install, because the tests use Node's built-in test runner.\n\n## The fixes\n\n| # | Bug | Fix |\n|---|-----|-----|\n| 1 | `addItem` accepted negative values | It now throws a `RangeError` unless the price is a finite number \u2265 0 and the quantity is a positive integer. This also stops `\"3\"` being glued onto an existing quantity as text instead of added. |\n| 2 | `removeItem` used `i.name = name` | That line renamed every item and removed nothing. It's now `i.name !== name`. The method also returns `true` if something was removed and `false` if the item wasn't there. |\n| 3 | `getTotal` loop used `<=` | Changed to `<`. The old loop read one slot past the end, so `getTotal()` crashed on every cart, including an empty one. |\n| 4 | Discount was added to the total | It's now subtracted: `total - total * discount / 100`. |\n| 5 | `applyDiscount` accepted any value | It now throws a `RangeError` unless the value is a finite number from 0 to 100. A rejected value leaves the previous discount in place. |\n\n## Tests\nTests are grouped by method: adding, removing, totals, discounts and item count. A separate `regression tests` section has one block per bug.\n\nIn the original code, bug 3 crashes `getTotal()` before the discount line runs, so bug 4 was hidden. To prove each regression block catches its own bug, I put each bug back on its own into the fixed code. Every time, that bug's block failed. Bugs 3 and 4 also fail other blocks, which is expected because those tests call `getTotal()`.\n\n## Choices I made\nYou may want to change these:\n- **Price 0 is allowed** (free items). **Quantity must be a whole number of at least 1**, so 0 and 1.5 are rejected.\n- **Removing an item that isn't in the cart returns `false`** rather than throwing an error.\n- **All invalid input throws `RangeError`**, including wrong types like `\"10\"` or `null`.\n\nI haven't committed anything; the new files are untracked.",
